                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   “Just so the record reflects that I tried to make you rich.” He
grinned, and the grin pulled some weight out of the room. “Sofía’s
gonna make a spreadsheet. It’s gonna have tabs.”



The 4 came through at 11:12.
   I don’t know why that one was different. They come every few
minutes. I’d stopped hearing them. But this one hit the curve up the
block and screamed against the rail, metal on metal, at the exact second
I had a latte going, and the two sounds stacked, the train’s scream and
the steamer’s whistle, and landed on the same note.
   The café tilted.
   Not the building; the building was shaking the way it always shakes.
My version of the room tilted, all of it sliding toward me. I had a con‐
tact mic puck stuck to the steel edge of the counter, where I’d been tun‐
ing Chismosa for Friday’s open mic, and one earbud in. The puck took
the train and the steamer and everything the old counter had ever been
near and fed it to my left ear. And my eyes, which had no say in it, fol‐
lowed.
   The tin ceiling stayed. The floor changed.
   Wooden bins ran the length of the room, packed with 45s in paper
sleeves, the heat on high, two box fans going, and a hand-lettered sign
over the register: HOY NO SE FÍA, MAÑANA SÍ. Today no credit, to‐
morrow yes. Behind the register stood a man with a mustache and a
good shirt, and he had Mr. Rodríguez’s eyebrows, half a century early.
A tiny radio on the shelf was playing a mambo like it owed him money.
On the window, taped crooked, was a handwritten flyer: BACK TO
SCHOOL JAM — SEDGWICK — SAT.
   A kid was at the counter. Fifteen, maybe sixteen, shell-toe Adidas, a
milk crate on his hip. He put two copies of the same record on the
counter.
